 ],
 "literal": "I thought, did I not, myself, having thought? / I pondered, did I not, greatly, having pondered? / I was bewildered, did I not, at the world's nature? / Tall kadamba tree's branch-touching abundant flood / having baled out to drink, like it went, / so very great love, having returned, taking hold at the end!",
 "translation": "Did I not think of her, thinking again and again? Did I not ponder deeply, pondering over and over? Did I not marvel at the way of the world? Like an abundant flood that touches the branches of tall kadamba trees, which disappears completely when baled out to be drunk, this immense love of mine has returned to engulf me at the very end.",
 "modern": "நான் அவளை மீண்டும் மீண்டும் நினைக்கவில்லையா? மிகவும் ஆழமாகச் சிந்திக்கவில்லையா? இந்த உலகத்தின் இயல்பைக் கண்டு வியப்படையவில்லையா? உயர்ந்த கடம்ப மரத்தின் கிளைகளைத் தொட்டு ஓடும் பெருவெள்ளத்தை இறைத்துக் குடிப்பதற்காகச் சென்றது போல, எனது இவ்வளவு பெரிய காம நோய் மீண்டும் வந்து என்னை முழுமையாகப் பற்றிக்கொண்டதே!",
 "explanation": "The hero is speaking to himself upon returning to his beloved during the rainy season. He reflects on how he tried to suppress his immense love while away, thinking he could manage it, but it has returned with overwhelming force. The poem belongs to Mullai tiṇai, which represents patient waiting and the return of the lover. The image of a massive flood touching the branches of tall kadamba trees represents the overwhelming nature of his love. Just as a flood disappears when baled out to be drunk, his attempts to control his passion only led to it returning to consume him entirely at the end of his journey.",
